Logical analysis of mathematical statements:
    
1. truth table and negation:
    If A is a statement, the negation "¬" is called a logical operator

    The statement "¬A" is called a logical expressions
    
    The truth table shows all the outputs of the statement and logical expression
        Ex. __________
           |  A | ¬A  |
           |  T |  F  |
           | _F_|__T__|

    note: in the truth table, we usually consider statement A as a variable which may has T or F value. Based on this, sometimes we may
        call A as statement variable.

2. conjunction and disjunction
    conjunction - means connects together -> and 
    disjunction - opposite conjunction -> or

    Compound statement:
        compound statement is a statement composed of several individual statements. each of which is called compound statement

    Conjunction:
        The truth value for A and B, written sybomatically by A ∧ B, is defined by the truth table:
            ___________________
           |__A_|__B__|__A∧B___|
           |__T_|__T__|___T____|
           |__F_|__F__|___F____|
           |__T_|__F__|___F____|
           |__F_|__T__|___F____|

        Ex. (1+2=3) ∧ (3+1=4) -> true

    Disjunction:
        The truth value for A and B, written sybomatically by A ∨ B, is defined by the truth table:
            ___________________
           |__A_|__B__|__A∨B___|
           |__T_|__T__|___T____|
           |__F_|__F__|___F____|
           |__T_|__F__|___T____|
           |__F_|__T__|___T____|

        Ex. (1+2 != 3) or (1+2 = 3) -> true

    Some laws about conjunction and disjunction:
        De morgan's law:
            ¬(A∧B)≡¬(A)∨¬(B)
            ¬(A∨B)≡¬(A)∧¬(B)

        Commutative law:
            A∨B ≡ B∨A
            A∧B ≡ B∧A

        associatative law:
            A∧(B∧C) ≡ (A∧B)∧C
            A∨(B∨C) ≡ (A∨B)∨C

        distributive law:
            A ∧ (B ∨ C) ≡(A ∧ B) ∨ (A ∧ C)
            A ∨ (B ∧ C) ≡(A ∨ B) ∧ (A ∨ C)

    


    